(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-nephrology-urinary-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Nephrology / Urinary System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 2)","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which organ of the urinary system is considered the primary organ, performing filtration?","choices":["The ureter","The kidney","The bladder","The urethra"],"answer":1,"exp":"The kidney is the primary urinary organ, performing filtration of the blood and converting filtrate into urine; the ureters, bladder, and urethra merely transport and store what the kidneys produce. — Slide 3 (Organs Involved)"},{"q":"The collective ureters, bladder, and urethra (the urinary tract) function primarily to:","choices":["Filter and cleanse blood plasma","Transport urine out of the body","Secrete the erythropoietin hormone","Concentrate the filtrate"],"answer":1,"exp":"The urinary tract (ureters, bladder, urethra) functions primarily to transport urine out of the body once the kidneys have already produced it. — Slide 4 (Systems Involved)"},{"q":"The primary functional role of the kidneys is to:","choices":["Produce reproductive hormones such as testosterone and estrogen for the gonads","Store urine in a distensible reservoir until voluntary micturition occurs","Transport urine through a muscular conduit out to the external environment","Filter waste products from the bloodstream and convert filtrate into urine"],"answer":3,"exp":"The kidneys' primary role is to filter waste products from the bloodstream and convert the filtrate into urine. — Slide 3 (Organs Involved)"},{"q":"Which sequence lists the tissues surrounding the kidney from deep (nearest the kidney) to superficial?","choices":["Perinephric fat → fibrous capsule → peritoneum → renal fascia","Peritoneum → renal fascia → perinephric fat → fibrous capsule","Renal fascia → fibrous capsule → paranephric fat → perinephric fat","Fibrous capsule → perinephric fat → renal fascia → paranephric fat → peritoneum"],"answer":3,"exp":"From deep to superficial: fibrous (renal) capsule, perinephric (adipose) fat, renal fascia, paranephric fat, then peritoneum. — Slide 11 (Surrounding Tissues)"},{"q":"The concave medial border of the kidney where vessels and the renal pelvis enter/exit is the:","choices":["Renal column","Renal papilla","Hilum","Major calyx"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hilum is the concave medial border of the kidney through which vessels, nerves, and the renal pelvis enter and exit; it leads internally to the renal sinus. — Slide 10 (Gross and Sectional Anatomy)"},{"q":"The renal medulla is divided into cone-shaped structures called renal pyramids, whose apex is known as the:","choices":["Renal arch","Renal column","Renal papilla","Renal sinus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each renal pyramid's apex, which projects toward a minor calyx, is called the renal papilla. — Slide 14 (Coronal Plane Sections)"},{"q":"The base of each renal pyramid is oriented toward which region of the kidney?","choices":["The cortex","The renal sinus","The hilum","The minor calyx"],"answer":0,"exp":"The base of each renal pyramid abuts the renal cortex, while its apex (the renal papilla) points toward a minor calyx. — Slide 14 (Coronal Plane Sections)"},{"q":"The renal capsule (fibrous capsule) is composed of which tissue?","choices":["Simple squamous epithelium","Dense irregular connective tissue","Smooth muscle found in the ureter wall","Adipose tissue surrounding the kidney"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fibrous (renal) capsule is dense irregular connective tissue that directly covers the outer cortex. — Slide 13 (Surrounding Tissues)"},{"q":"Immediately after the renal artery, blood enters which set of arteries as it progresses toward the cortex?","choices":["Segmental → lobar → interlobar arteries","Arcuate → interlobular → afferent arterioles","Interlobular → segmental → arcuate arteries","Efferent → peritubular → vasa recta"],"answer":0,"exp":"After the renal artery, blood flows through segmental → lobar → interlobar arteries, then arcuate → interlobular → afferent arteriole → glomerulus. — Slide 16 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Blood exits the glomerulus through which vessel (rather than a vein)?","choices":["The efferent arteriole","The interlobular vein","The cortical arcuate vein","The main renal vein itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Uniquely among capillary beds, blood leaves the glomerulus via a second arteriole — the efferent arteriole — rather than a vein. — Slides 16, 18 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"The efferent arteriole branches into two capillary types responsible for solute exchange: the peritubular capillaries and the:","choices":["Portal capillaries","Glomerular capillaries","Fenestrated cortical sinusoids","Vasa recta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Efferent arterioles form peritubular capillaries (juxtaposed to the nephron) and the vasa recta, which perform solute/molecule exchange. — Slide 18 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Which sequence traces venous return from the vasa recta to the IVC?","choices":["Arcuate → interlobular → segmental → renal vein → IVC","Interlobular → arcuate → interlobar → renal vein → IVC","Interlobar → interlobular → arcuate → renal vein → IVC","Renal vein → arcuate → interlobar → IVC"],"answer":1,"exp":"Venous return: vasa recta → interlobular vein → arcuate vein → interlobar vein → renal vein → inferior vena cava. — Slide 18 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"The two components of the renal corpuscle are the glomerulus and the:","choices":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Glomerular (Bowman's) capsule","The loop of Henle segment","The collecting duct segment"],"answer":1,"exp":"The renal corpuscle consists of the glomerulus and the glomerular (Bowman's) capsule. — Slide 29 (Nephron Structures)"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly orders filtrate flow through the nephron tubules?","choices":["PCT → DCT → loop of Henle → ascending limb → collecting duct","DCT → PCT → descending limb → ascending limb → collecting duct","PCT → descending limb → loop of Henle → ascending limb → DCT → collecting duct","Loop of Henle → PCT → ascending limb → DCT → collecting duct"],"answer":2,"exp":"Filtrate flows: Bowman's capsule → PCT → descending limb → loop of Henle → ascending limb → DCT → collecting duct. — Slide 22 (Urinary/Filtrate Flow)"},{"q":"Juxtamedullary (medullary) nephrons, comprising ~15% of nephrons, are distinguished by:","choices":["Location confined to the outer cortical edge","Short loops paired with peritubular capillaries","Complete absence of a glomerulus within the renal corpuscle","Long nephron loops extending into the medulla, with vasa recta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Juxtamedullary nephrons (~15%) are distinguished by long loops of Henle extending deep into the medulla, paired with vasa recta that allow them to concentrate urine. — Slide 30 (Types of Nephrons)"},{"q":"Which nephron type produces 'standard' urine and sits near the peripheral edge of the cortex?","choices":["Papillary nephrons","Juxtamedullary nephrons","Medullary nephrons","Cortical nephrons"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cortical nephrons (~85%) sit near the peripheral edge of the cortex and produce 'standard,' non-concentrated urine. — Slide 30 (Types of Nephrons)"},{"q":"The proximal convoluted tubule begins at which structure?","choices":["The tubular pole of the renal corpuscle","The ending point of the loop of Henle","The start of the collecting duct itself","The opening of the minor calyx itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"The PCT begins at the tubular pole of the renal corpuscle and ends where the thick descending limb of the loop of Henle begins. — Slide 38 (Proximal Convoluted Tubule)"},{"q":"The distal convoluted tubule begins at the end of the thick ascending limb and lies adjacent to which structure?","choices":["The minor calyx located at the papilla","The afferent arteriole (juxtaglomerular apparatus)","The renal papilla at the pyramid's apex","The collecting duct system exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"The DCT begins at the end of the thick ascending limb and lies adjacent to the afferent arteriole, forming part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus. — Slide 40 (Distal Convoluted Tubule)"},{"q":"The kidneys are classified as which type of organ with respect to the peritoneum?","choices":["Retroperitoneal","Intraperitoneal","Subperitoneal","Suspended by mesentery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The kidneys are retroperitoneal organs anchored to the posterior body wall behind the peritoneum. — Slides 6, 13 (Kidney Location)"},{"q":"The right kidney typically spans which vertebral levels?","choices":["T6 to T9","T8 to T10","L3 to L5","T12 to L3"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right kidney typically spans about T12 to L3, sitting slightly lower than the left because the liver displaces it inferiorly. — Slide 6 (Kidney Location)"},{"q":"The left renal vein is distinctive because it receives which additional vein?","choices":["The azygos vein","The right suprarenal vein","The left gonadal vein","The portal vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The left renal vein is distinctive because it also receives the left gonadal vein (and left suprarenal vein) before draining into the IVC. — Slide 6 (Kidney Location)"},{"q":"The ureters are described as passing 'water under the bridge,' meaning the ureter travels deep (inferior) to which vessels?","choices":["The inferior mesenteric artery alone","The renal artery and vein directly","The common iliac artery exclusively","The testicular or ovarian artery and vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"The 'water under the bridge' relationship describes the ureter passing deep (inferior) to the gonadal (testicular/ovarian) vessels bilaterally. — Slide 48 ('Water under the bridge')"},{"q":"The parietal layer of the glomerular (Bowman's) capsule is lined by which epithelium?","choices":["Simple cuboidal epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Podocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The parietal (outer) layer of Bowman's capsule is simple squamous epithelium; the visceral (inner) layer consists of podocytes. — Slide 35 (Renal Corpuscle)"},{"q":"The specialized visceral epithelial cells that wrap the glomerular capillaries with foot processes (pedicels) are the:","choices":["Podocytes","Macula densa cells","Juxtaglomerular cells","Mesangial cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Podocytes are the specialized visceral epithelial cells that wrap the glomerular capillaries with foot processes (pedicels), creating filtration slits. — Slides 34–35 (Filtration Membranes / Renal Corpuscle)"},{"q":"The proximal convoluted tubule is lined by which epithelium?","choices":["Transitional epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Stratified columnar epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"The PCT is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium. — Slide 38 (Proximal Convoluted Tubule)"},{"q":"The ureters and bladder are lined by which epithelium, which allows them to stretch?","choices":["Simple cuboidal epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Transitional epithelium lines the ureters and bladder, allowing distension as urine volume changes. — Slides 45, 53 (Ureter Walls / Bladder)"},{"q":"With respect to the peritoneum, the urinary bladder is best described as:","choices":["Suspended freely by its own mesentery","Completely intraperitoneal in position","Fully retroperitoneal like the kidney","Non-peritoneal except for its superior wall"],"answer":3,"exp":"The urinary bladder is a non-peritoneal organ except for its superior wall, which is covered by peritoneum — a distinction that affects where bladder pain is sensed. — Slide 49 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The distal convoluted tubule contains a cluster of cells that sense tubular NaCl; these cells are the:","choices":["Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells","Podocytes","Macula densa","Parietal squamous cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"The macula densa is a cluster of cells in the DCT that senses NaCl concentration as part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus. — Slide 36 (Juxtaglomerular Apparatus)"},{"q":"The juxtaglomerular (granular) cells, modified smooth muscle of the afferent arteriole, synthesize and secrete which substance?","choices":["Renin","Erythropoietin","Calcitriol","Aldosterone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells secrete renin in response to decreased BP or macula densa stimulation. — Slide 36 (Juxtaglomerular Apparatus)"},{"q":"The kidney averages approximately what length?","choices":["20 cm","4 cm","11 cm","30 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The average kidney is approximately 11 cm in length. — Slide 6 (Kidney Location)"}]};
